WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
Why do you want to get rid of birthright citizenship?

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:02.360
And how, by the way?

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:03.640
Because you know it's in the Constitution.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:04.800
I'm so glad you brought this up.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:05.080
Yes.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:05.880
Because it was one of my-

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:06.400
The 14th.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:06.760
14th.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:08.160
I mentioned it to a bunch of your friends,

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.720
and nobody wanted to engage in the 14th.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.080
Yeah.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.320
Let's talk about the 14th Amendment.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:11.820
Exactly.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.440
The Citizenship Clause, which says that if you're born here,

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.040
you're a citizen of the United States.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:16.559
Yes.

00:00:16.559 --> 00:00:18.360
And I chose this because it's an extension

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.360
of the original debate and the immigration debate.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:20.860
OK.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.040
I think birthright citizenship, first of all,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.160
when it was originally argued among the people who were

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.280
actually voting on the clause, it

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.520
was argued that the birthright citizenship was not

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.360
automatically extended to everybody

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:36.320
who was just born here.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.080
And we have a long history of that as well,

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:39.720
for example, with the Native Americans,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.439
who had particular laws that applied solely to them.

00:00:42.439 --> 00:00:42.840
Yeah.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.520
What I think we're seeing in this country

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.279
is not everybody is so similarly

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:49.480
under the jurisdiction thereof of the United States, right?

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.720
And by allowing for birthright citizenship

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.380
and not interpreting the Constitution

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.119
as we are legally allowed to and restrict

00:00:56.119 --> 00:00:58.439
the automatic citizenship of anybody who's born here,

00:00:58.439 --> 00:01:00.720
we actually remove the issue, for example,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:01.720
of family separation.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.560
Because there is no incentive of a family

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.059
to come over to this country illegally

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:07.080
and then have a child, which is contributing

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.400
to the awful situation we have in America of breaking up

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.040
families.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.440
This is not something that I imagine you or I agree with.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:12.800
No.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.220
The issue is birthright citizenship

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.040
has incentivized that.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.120
So even if I were to concede that in some cases

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.480
birthright citizenship has incentivized that,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.279
I don't like the phrase anchor babies,

00:01:23.279 --> 00:01:25.080
but I'm not naive enough to pretend

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.919
that there are not immigrants who come here

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:29.199
and time the pregnancy in order to get.

00:01:29.199 --> 00:01:30.360
You and I can agree on that.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:30.919
Yeah.

00:01:30.919 --> 00:01:32.800
But that doesn't mean you then rip up

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.639
a constitutional principle that goes back centuries,

00:01:35.639 --> 00:01:37.480
goes back before the 14th Amendment.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.360
14th Amendment came about, I think, in 1868,

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.800
if memory serves me correctly.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.720
But the principle underlying it is actually

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.239
from my other country, the UK, ancient English common law.

00:01:47.239 --> 00:01:48.480
So this was always the case.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.959
The 14th Amendment, as you know, because you seem well-read,

00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:53.160
came along and codified what was already

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.080
a principle in American and English law

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.279
that if you're born in the country, you are a citizen.

00:01:57.279 --> 00:01:58.639
Now, you mentioned the point about jurisdiction.

00:01:58.639 --> 00:02:00.040
There is that little bit in there

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.320
where it says subject to the jurisdiction thereof.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.599
That in the debate, if you go back and read the debate

00:02:04.599 --> 00:02:06.320
that the senators had at the time,

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:07.760
they were very explicit that that

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.279
was to do with foreign ministers and foreign ambassadors.

00:02:10.279 --> 00:02:11.800
Diplomats living in embassies, their kids

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:12.720
shouldn't be citizens.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.399
Obviously, they're here on their own.

00:02:14.399 --> 00:02:17.559
The Argentine embassy in DC is Argentine soil.

00:02:17.559 --> 00:02:18.520
So we accept all that.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:19.479
Absolutamente, amigo.

00:02:19.479 --> 00:02:22.320
But when they were asked about Chinese immigrants,

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.039
Senator Trumbull, who was, I think,

00:02:24.039 --> 00:02:26.320
the chair of the Judiciary Committee back in the 1860s

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.600
during this debate, he said at the time,

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.559
Chinese immigrants' children, they are citizens.

00:02:31.559 --> 00:02:34.080
In fact, 1898, as you well know, we

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.759
get Wong Kim Ark, Supreme Court case, Wong Kim

00:02:36.759 --> 00:02:38.520
Ark versus the United States.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.600
And the Supreme Court says, yes, even

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.000
the child of undocumented Chinese immigrants

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.279
is an American citizen.

00:02:44.279 --> 00:02:46.199
So this is a principle going back centuries.

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:47.960
So while I can agree with you that there

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.160
are cases that abuse the 14th Amendment,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:51.960
that there are issues of family separation,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.279
I'm not going to rip up a constitutional principle that

00:02:54.279 --> 00:02:56.080
goes back centuries and is backed

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.199
by a 127-year-old Supreme Court president

00:02:59.199 --> 00:03:00.839
because Donald Trump is looking for ways

00:03:00.839 --> 00:03:02.759
to cut the number of brown and black people

00:03:02.759 --> 00:03:03.679
in the United States.

00:03:03.679 --> 00:03:04.960
If you don't mind me interrupting,

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.380
this is where I think it's important as well

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.800
to go back to what we talked about with the Constitution.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.520
When we're analyzing, and admittedly, I'm

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.199
not as interested in the legal argument.

00:03:13.199 --> 00:03:16.279
There's obviously many ways to interpret the Constitution.

00:03:16.279 --> 00:03:18.520
But I don't think, especially among conservatives,

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:21.360
that it is positive to use the Constitution

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:22.199
to dismantle it.

00:03:22.199 --> 00:03:23.880
When we analyze the original context,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.360
like you were saying, in the late 19th century,

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.559
our immigration law was absolutely different,

00:03:28.559 --> 00:03:31.639
including in respect with the Irish and Chinese immigrants

00:03:31.639 --> 00:03:32.559
that were coming over.

00:03:32.559 --> 00:03:34.279
It was absolutely different, changed.

00:03:34.279 --> 00:03:35.600
We had open borders, my friend.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.800
Some people in this room would hate it.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:37.039
But hold on.

00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:39.119
We had open borders for half of American history.

00:03:39.119 --> 00:03:42.039
Well, I don't think that's very true.

00:03:42.039 --> 00:03:43.000
It's 100% true.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:43.679
Hold on.

00:03:43.679 --> 00:03:46.320
If you're comparing the open borders, which I don't concede

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.000
to, but if you're comparing the open borders prior to 1910

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.679
and prior to the Hart-Cellar Act,

00:03:51.679 --> 00:03:53.600
and you look at the amount of immigration,

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:54.679
and who was coming over.

00:03:54.679 --> 00:03:55.360
Oh, nice deflection.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.160
Amount is irrelevant.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:56.679
We did have an open border.

00:03:56.679 --> 00:03:57.559
It is absolutely relevant.

00:03:57.559 --> 00:03:58.080
Why?

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.399
Because the Constitution, like the founding father.

00:04:00.399 --> 00:04:00.880
So hold on.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.199
Your argument is, we had open borders,

00:04:02.199 --> 00:04:03.199
but there were fewer immigrants.

00:04:03.199 --> 00:04:04.320
I was about to answer.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.720
It's important to analyze the context wherein these laws

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.199
and parts of the Constitution arise.

00:04:08.199 --> 00:04:09.639
The original founding father said,

00:04:09.639 --> 00:04:12.000
the Constitution was made only for a moral and religious

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.160
people and is wholly inadequate to the government

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.000
of any other, which means the people that wrote this document

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.440
understood that it would not be able to properly

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:20.359
For secular people.

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:21.079
Let's just be clear on that.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:21.839
Oh, no, that's not true.

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:22.279
They were deists.

00:04:22.279 --> 00:04:22.959
A lot of them were super secular.

00:04:22.959 --> 00:04:24.200
No, the majority were deists.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:24.799
But they were secular.

00:04:24.799 --> 00:04:25.799
Secular doesn't mean atheist.

00:04:25.799 --> 00:04:26.359
You know what that means.

00:04:26.359 --> 00:04:26.959
I understand that.

00:04:26.959 --> 00:04:28.799
But deist doesn't mean secular or atheist.

00:04:28.799 --> 00:04:30.239
It means they believe in a God, but not a God that's

00:04:30.239 --> 00:04:30.559
particularly present.

00:04:30.559 --> 00:04:31.720
I didn't say anything about whether they're deist or theist.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:32.359
I said they're secular.

00:04:32.359 --> 00:04:33.079
OK, but secular and deist.

00:04:33.079 --> 00:04:34.480
It means you separate church and state.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:35.399
OK, well, let's talk.

00:04:35.399 --> 00:04:36.600
We could even talk about that.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:37.480
That's a perfect example.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:38.959
You're going to run out of your own time making your argument,

00:04:38.959 --> 00:04:39.600
which you haven't made so far.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.079
I understand.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.279
But the separation of church and state

00:04:41.279 --> 00:04:42.959
is now completely differently understood

00:04:42.959 --> 00:04:44.239
than originally proposed.

00:04:44.239 --> 00:04:46.079
Thomas Jefferson, who talks about the wall of separation,

00:04:46.079 --> 00:04:47.920
which doesn't even exist in the Constitution,

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.760
understood it in a different way.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.119
But it is a Supreme Court precedent, as you well know.

00:04:50.119 --> 00:04:50.640
Yeah, sure.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.600
But the precedent of the last 100 years, where

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:53.559
in the country has been completely

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:55.239
different from its founding, should not

00:04:55.239 --> 00:04:57.119
overrule the original intent of the people who

00:04:57.119 --> 00:04:59.279
wrote the document that you and I are both citing.

00:04:59.279 --> 00:05:00.079
Which means.

00:05:00.079 --> 00:05:02.559
But I'm not locked into what the people who wrote that.

00:05:02.559 --> 00:05:03.880
We've had amendments to the Constitution.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:04.320
You know that, right?

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:05.119
I understand that.

00:05:05.119 --> 00:05:06.640
And this is our fundamental disagreement,

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:07.600
is that I don't believe.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.679
You think you can read the minds of people in the 1770s.

00:05:09.679 --> 00:05:10.480
That's not true.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:11.079
And I'm stuck.

00:05:11.079 --> 00:05:12.399
I just believe this country ought

00:05:12.399 --> 00:05:14.200
to be more like the country we originally had.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:14.720
And I don't think.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:15.600
Great, that's your opinion.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:16.239
And you're entitled to it.

00:05:16.239 --> 00:05:16.799
I understand that.

00:05:16.799 --> 00:05:17.839
But on a factual basis.

00:05:17.839 --> 00:05:20.720
Well, let me try to see what you believe.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:21.640
OK.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:24.279
If we are voting on these constitutional amendments,

00:05:24.279 --> 00:05:27.040
if we are interpreting them, and our interpretation becomes

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.200
absolutely, let's say, a 180 from what

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:33.119
the original intent of the authors of that document is,

00:05:33.119 --> 00:05:35.239
are you in favor of that?

